@article{Altincekic2021,
   abstract = {The highly infectious disease COVID-19 caused by the Betacoronavirus SARS-CoV-2 poses a severe threat to humanity and demands the redirection of scientific efforts and criteria to organized research projects. The international COVID19-NMR consortium seeks to provide such new approaches by gathering scientific expertise worldwide. In particular, making available viral proteins and RNAs will pave the way to understanding the SARS-CoV-2 molecular components in detail. The research in COVID19-NMR and the resources provided through the consortium are fully disclosed to accelerate access and exploitation. NMR investigations of the viral molecular components are designated to provide the essential basis for further work, including macromolecular interaction studies and high-throughput drug screening. Here, we present the extensive catalog of a holistic SARS-CoV-2 protein preparation approach based on the consortium's collective efforts. We provide protocols for the large-scale production of more than 80% of all SARS-CoV-2 proteins or essential parts of them. Several of the proteins were produced in more than one laboratory, demonstrating the high interoperability between NMR groups worldwide. For the majority of proteins, we can produce isotope-labeled samples of HSQC-grade. Together with several NMR chemical shift assignments made publicly available on covid19-nmr.com, we here provide highly valuable resources for the production of SARS-CoV-2 proteins in isotope-labeled form. FU  - Goethe University (Corona funds); DFGGerman Research Foundation (DFG)European Commission [277478796, 277479031, 392682309, 452632086, 70653611, SCHL 2062/2-1]; state of Hesse (BMRZ); Fondazione CR Firenze (CERM); IWB-EFRE-program [20007375]; European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation program [871037]; JQYA at Goethe [2019/AS01]; University of New Hampshire; Federal Government of Germany; State of Thuringia; NIHUnited States Department of Health & Human ServicesNational Institutes of Health (NIH) - USA [2R01EY021514, R25 GM055246, R01GM123249]; NSFNational Science Foundation (NSF) [DMR-2002837, MCB2031269, RAPID 2030601]; NSF GRFPNational Science Foundation (NSF)NSF - Office of the Director (OD); HHMI Gilliam Fellowship; Latvian Council of ScienceLatvian Ministry of Education and Science [VPP-COVID 2020/1-0014]; INSPIRED project under the Action "Reinforcement of the Research and Innovation Infrastructure," - Operational Program "Competitiveness, Entrepreneurship and Innovation" (NSRF 2014-2020) [MIS 5002550]; EU (European Regional Development Fund)European Commission; Helmholtz societyHelmholtz Association; CNRSCentre National de la Recherche Scientifique (CNRS)European Commission; French National Research Agency(ANR)French National Research Agency (ANR) [NMRSCoV2-ORF8]; Fondation de la Recherche Medicale (FRM)Fondation pour la Recherche Medicale [NMR-SCoV2-ORF8]; Swiss National Science FoundationSwiss National Science Foundation (SNSF)European Commission [200020_188711]; Gunthard Stiftung fur Physikalische Chemie; ETH ZurichETH Zurich [ETH40 18 1]; SNF [31CA30_196256]; Krebsliga [KFS 4903 08 2019]; Agence Nationale de Recherche (France) RA-COVID SARS2NUCLEOPROTEINFrench National Research Agency (ANR); European Research CouncilEuropean Research Council (ERC)European Commission; Patto per il Sud della Regione Siciliana-CheMISt grant [CUP G77B17000110001]; FRISBI [ANR-10-INBS-05-02]; GRAL within the University Grenoble Alpes graduate school (Ecoles Universitaires de Recherche) CBH-EUR-GS [ANR-17-EURE0003]; NIH/NIAIDUnited States Department of Health & Human ServicesNational Institutes of Health (NIH) - USANIH National Institute of Allergy & Infectious Diseases (NIAID) [AI123498]; Spanish AEI-Ministry of Science and Innovation [RYC2019-026574-I]; "La Caixa" FoundationLa Caixa Foundation [100010434, LCR/BQ/PR19/11700003]; Carlos III Institute of HealthInstituto de Salud Carlos III [COV20/00764]; Spanish Ministry of Science and Innova